222 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (The ashtray holds historical significance as it reflects the lifestyle and domestic practices of the Abenis family, showing how household items were both functional and decorative. Its aesthetic significance comes from its design, which allowed it to serve as a decorative piece in the home, enhancing the visual appeal of living spaces. Socially, it demonstrates the relationship between households and their employees, as it was preserved and cherished by Mrs. Gemma Cacanog, highlighting the cultural value placed on everyday objects within domestic life.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
223 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (The ashtray is representative of mid-20th-century Filipino household artifacts, serving both functional and decorative purposes commonly found in homes of that era. While ashtrays were once commonplace, this particular piece is rare because it transitioned from a purely functional object to a cherished decorative item and has been preserved along with its history. Beyond its physical presence, the ashtray holds strong interpretative potential, offering insights into domestic culture, aesthetic preferences, and the social dynamics between households and their employees, making it a valuable artifact for the study of material culture and social history.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
